finding: Reviewed HEAD 96fe9a074da38e789e7f083fa3422d185bc492bc independently. Findings: 1. src/extras/multiplayer/index.ts:206-214,240-246; link.ts:1-5: replication drains the entire application's Connection, discarding other messages, with no message/channel discriminator. A four-byte input/operation is interpreted as an acknowledgement. Reproduced: record tick 0; client sends four zero bytes before receiving the image; sender.flush deletes the pending image without ever delivering it; record tick 1; receiver stays confirmed=-1 forever. Non-four-byte client messages are simply lost. This seam cannot carry stage 4's inputs/operations on the same connection without a demultiplexer/protocol contract, unlike the referents' explicit message channels. Acknowledgement state can claim a baseline the client never had. 2. src/extras/multiplayer/replication.test.ts:118-125: the entity-reference oracle derives its expected reference from decoded rows rather than authoritative intent, and skips its positive assertion when the target is zero. Independently changed capture's entity branch (index.ts:77) to always emit zero: ALL SIX multiplayer tests still passed (1346 assertions). Restored the mutation and verified a clean worktree. This fails the required test sensitivity; the suite cannot prove even ordinary non-null mapping, let alone references crossing disclosure/spawn boundaries. 3. src/extras/multiplayer/index.ts:88-113: all field payloads use f64 without earning the wire cost. f32 values and decoded f16 values are exactly representable in f32, integer fields in their declared 32 bits, and entity references have already become u32 wire ids. Measured ten rows with first-person's common Transform, GlobalTransform, Body and Shape components: 92 scalar lanes per body; image 11,460 bytes (1,144/entity plus 20 packet bytes), unchanged frame 420 bytes. Keeping the same per-lane index/framing but using 32-bit values would make that image 7,780 bytes: payload doubles; entire image is 47.3% larger. This excludes avatar/gameplay-specific fields and retries. The source's preservation comment is not a reason to double these representable values; no spike-scale evidence earns it. Examined: required premise, stages 3-8, design notes, Multiplayer referents and CONTRIBUTING sections; full multiplayer implementation/tests, exports/import boundary, first-person common component schemas, spike disclosure schema, and public channel precedents. Independently exercised a reference to an undisclosed/not-yet-disclosed entity, subsequent disclosure/spawn, and destruction: null, mapped client eid, null as expected. Read baseline/ACK ordering and image/retry logic; no separate honest replication-only loss/reordering counterexample found. Core remains unchanged and fields() is public, not @internal. Replicate/rules/schema are plain selection/schema data with a sensible precedent; same-connection routing above is the demonstrated future-stage blocker, not a blanket objection to per-recipient history objects. Checks: bun test src/extras/multiplayer: 6 pass, 1424 assertions on original code. bun test ./scripts/check-imports.node.ts ./scripts/check-package.node.ts: 10 pass, 19 assertions. Temporary mutation restored; git diff empty. Notes/uncertainty: record() accepts gapped ticks but receiver requires every consecutive tick (record 5,7 leaves confirmed at 5); documented once-per-tick usage avoids this, but enforcing/documenting contiguity would clarify the public contract. Prediction restore/replay, native socket behavior, avatar-specific wire totals, throughput/allocation profiling and exhaustive seed coverage were not established by this review. Later stages remain necessary; the existing confirmation callback alone is not proof of physics correction/replay integration.
